PS5 HFR1 / CS2 Nuke / RADV graphics descriptor SGPR diagnostic
2026-11-07, offline demo at 1x, first-person slot 9, demo seek tick 10048 (~02:38).
HFR1 7.2.3-ps5-hfr1, Radeon GFX1013, 1920x1080 at 240 Hz, stock MP1,
CPUs 0-15, private Mesa 26.0.8 A2-O2-noalias build, split APU heap, immediate WSI.
The private observation-only ICD was selected exclusively in the -insecure CS2 process.
Shader caches were private. Game and Steam config restoration was verified byte-for-byte.

Inclusion: GFX graphics command buffers begun and ended during [resume+1s,resume+16s).
The resume timestamp is anchored to the fresh game log marker and includes its logging delay.
Counters describe CPU command recording in PM4 IBs. They do not measure GPU bus traffic,
submitted/replayed buffer multiplicity, push constants, or dynamic/vertex descriptors.
A complete packet match requires all destination SH register dwords to match values known
from preceding PM4 in the same IB. Known interfering writes and unknown state invalidate
history. The result is a lower bound under the observer's PM4 model, not proof that a
production driver can discard every counted packet safely.
A bind-associated packet follows at least one graphics BindPipeline since the previous
observed descriptor flush of that command buffer. The category is temporal, not causal.
Raw matches ignore intervening non-descriptor writes and are reported separately.

V1 established the replay window with X11 Flip, but treated a conditional PM4 packet as
opaque for the rest of the command buffer, so its shadow coverage was too low. Intermediate
attempts that did not establish a complete timed window were excluded. The refined run
has the fixed replay tick/POV and full duration, but X11 reported Copy throughout the
scored window. This invalidated the Flip requirement for a comparable display benchmark.
It remains a descriptor-recording diagnostic. No clean FPS/P99 A/B/A or optimization
patch was run, and the counter build never removed an emitted command.
No new MCE or amdgpu fault/timeout/reset was found in the kernel log during the final run.

